  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer for PhD holders</h2>

  <p>
    A political science PhD is not a one-way ticket into a lecture hall. It is a credential in
    original research, sustained argument, and deep subject command — a credential that genuinely
    feeds five different career tracks, each with its own pay structure, timeline, and risk.
  </p>

  <p>
    The confusion for most PhD holders is not a shortage of options. It is that the default advice —
    "keep clearing NET, keep applying for assistant professor posts" — treats academia as the only
    serious destination, when three of the five tracks below can pay as well or better, faster.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-different">Why a PhD changes the decision</h2>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Why this is a different question than "what can a political science graduate do"</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>You have already spent 4-7+ years and cleared UGC-NET/JRF — a fresh graduate is choosing whether to start that; you are choosing what to do with it now that it is done.</li>
      <li>Your remaining UPSC attempts and age-limit window are almost certainly smaller than a fresh graduate's, simply because the doctorate took years.</li>
      <li>You can enter a think tank at Fellow or Senior Fellow level, not the entry research-associate rung most fresh graduates start at.</li>
      <li>Ad-hoc and guest-faculty pay, and the wait for a permanent post, are a real near-term reality specific to people who have already finished a PhD, not a hypothetical future problem.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      A fresh graduate's guide correctly spends most of its time on whether to do a PhD at all. This
      one assumes that decision is already behind you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="tracks">The 5 real tracks, ranked by growth — not by prestige</h2>

  <p>
    The order below follows real scalability and growth headroom as you gain reputation and proof, not
    which title sounds most respectable at a family gathering. The tracks with real ownership or an
    uncapped pay structure come first; the tracks locked to a fixed government or UGC scale come last,
    regardless of how prestigious they sound.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {trackCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.badge}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.name}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
        <div class="feature-card__tags">
          {item.tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Academia sits last on this list, not first, and that is deliberate. It is real, valuable work
      for people who genuinely love research and teaching. But its pay is fixed by a UGC scale, its
      permanent-post market has not kept pace with the number of qualified PhDs, and the tracks above
      it can reach a comparable or higher ceiling faster for someone willing to build a public research
      record rather than wait for a selection committee.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="consulting">Track 1: independent research consulting and a commentary platform</h2>

  <p>
    This is the track most PhD holders underuse, because academic training rewards writing for three
    peer reviewers, not for a paying client or a public audience. It is also the one with the most
    genuine room to scale. Political consultancies, corporate government-affairs teams, and media
    houses all pay for a named specialist who can turn deep subject knowledge into a client-ready
    brief, a data-backed analysis, or a public explainer, on a retainer or project basis.
  </p>

  <p>
    A related and often underrated version: building your own research platform — a policy newsletter,
    a data-driven explainer series, or a niche consulting practice in your exact sub-field (electoral
    behaviour, federalism, foreign policy of a specific region, and so on) — that you own outright
    instead of publishing only inside academic journals few outside your field will ever read.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Why this scales when the others don't:</strong> you set your own pricing instead of
      sitting on a UGC or government pay scale, demand repeats with every election cycle, policy
      debate, or regulatory shift rather than depending on one employer's budget, and once you have a
      track record you can bring on research assistants instead of doing every hour yourself. A public
      platform built the same way carries the identical advantage — reach and reputation that a
      journal paywall or a single university's payroll cannot cap.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The entry route is rarely a cold pitch straight after the viva. Most PhD holders build this by
    publishing one or two public-facing pieces — an op-ed, a data-backed explainer, a policy brief
    written to a real institution's format — that translate the dissertation into something a client
    or an editor outside academia can actually use and find.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are seriously weighing the leap into independent consulting rather than staying inside an
    institution, read
    <a href="/blog/freelancing-business/how-to-become-an-independent-consultant-india/">how to become an independent consultant in India</a>
    for the readiness test before you leave a stable stipend or salary for retainer clients.
  </p>

  <h2 id="think-tanks">Track 2: senior think-tank and policy research roles</h2>

  <p>
    Research associate and policy analyst roles at institutions such as the Observer Research
    Foundation, Carnegie India, the Centre for Policy Research, the Institute for Defence Studies and
    Analyses, and the Takshashila Institution are usually where a fresh graduate enters this world. A
    PhD holder in political science is a different candidate: the doctorate, combined with published
    work, commonly qualifies you to enter directly at Fellow or Senior Fellow level, skipping the
    entry rung most fresh graduates start on.
  </p>

  <p>
    That matters for pay and influence both. A Fellow is expected to publish independently, brief
    policymakers or media directly, and often supervise junior researchers — work that compounds into
    consulting invitations, media commentary requests, and advisory-board seats over a few years, none
    of which show up in a public salary figure but genuinely raise your ceiling.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Reliable public pay figures at Senior Fellow or Director level are scarce because most
      institutions negotiate senior compensation individually rather than publishing a scale. Do not
      let that opacity push you toward assuming the pay is low — treat it as a reason to ask directly
      and compare offers, not as evidence the ceiling is capped the way a UGC scale is.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For the broader landscape of research-adjacent careers this overlaps with, including roles that do
    not require a doctorate at all, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-in-research-for-humanities-students-india/">a career in research for humanities students in India</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="international">Track 3: international bodies and multilateral organisations</h2>

  <p>
    UN-system agencies, multilateral development banks, and large international NGOs hire political
    science PhD holders for policy analysis, governance, peacebuilding, and development-research roles
    that a bachelor's or Master's degree alone usually cannot access. The realistic entry routes are a
    Junior Professional Officer or equivalent early-career programme, a research fellowship inside one
    of these institutions, or a few years of field or research experience that builds toward a P-2 or
    P-3 professional post.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest trade-off: pay on the UN P-scale, once you are in, often runs meaningfully above
    equivalent Indian academic or government pay, especially once post-adjustment for the duty station
    is added. But the number of seats is genuinely small relative to the number of qualified
    applicants worldwide, and most entry routes expect either a specific home-government sponsorship,
    a competitive fellowship, or years of prior field work — rarely a direct hire straight after the
    viva.
  </p>

  <p>
    A working foreign language relevant to your specialisation and region, plus prior fieldwork or an
    internship inside the UN system, ILO, World Bank, or a comparable body, genuinely changes your
    odds here more than the PhD title alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="civil-services">Track 4: UPSC and civil services with a research edge</h2>

  <p>
    A PhD can sharpen your optional-subject depth and interview performance for the UPSC Civil
    Services Examination considerably. What it cannot change is the exam's structure or the fixed
    government pay scale on the other side of it.
  </p>

