  </div>

  <p class="table-note">
    Confirm the current cycle's exact age, attempt, and category rules on the
    <a href="https://upsc.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official UPSC website</a>
    before planning a year of preparation around them.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take: the age-limit squeeze a PhD specifically creates</p>
    <p>
      A political science PhD typically takes four to seven years, sometimes longer, after a Master's
      degree. Started at 23-24, that can mean finishing anywhere from the late 20s to the mid-30s.
      For a general-category candidate, that arithmetic can leave very few attempts, or none, by the
      time the doctorate is done. This is not a reason to rule the exam out — it is a reason to check
      your exact remaining attempts and age window honestly before committing a year of preparation to
      it, the same way you would check funding before starting a PhD.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    State Public Service Commissions run a parallel, somewhat less competitive route with their own
    age and attempt rules that vary by state — worth checking specifically if the national exam's
    window has already narrowed for you. If UPSC or a state exam does not work out, or you decide the
    age math no longer favours it, read
    <a href="/blog/career-options/upsc-alternative-careers-for-humanities-students-india/">UPSC alternative careers for humanities students</a>
    and
    <a href="/blog/government-jobs/what-to-do-after-failed-upsc-attempts-india/">what to do after failed UPSC attempts in India</a>
    for the honest next step.
  </p>

  <h2 id="academia">Track 5: academia — from PhD to a permanent post</h2>

  <p>
    If you genuinely love the research and teaching itself, academia is real, valuable work, and it is
    the path you have already started. It is also the track with the longest and most uncertain
    runway from where you stand right now, because finishing the PhD does not finish the competition —
    it restarts it in a different, less discussed form.
  </p>

  <p>
    Parliamentary replies and UGC data over recent years have repeatedly shown a large number of
    sanctioned assistant professor posts vacant across central and state universities, with ad-hoc and
    guest faculty covering the teaching load for years at a stretch while permanent recruitment moves
    slowly, department by department, subject by subject. Political science departments are not
    exempt from this pattern.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hiring-pipeline">How assistant professor hiring actually works, stage by stage</h2>

  <p>
    Most PhD holders know they need to clear UGC-NET/JRF and finish the doctorate. Far fewer have
    mapped the actual hiring process for a permanent post, which typically runs through four separate
    stages, each of which can eliminate a genuinely qualified candidate.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    <div class="checkpoint">
      <div class="checkpoint__num">01</div>
      <div class="checkpoint__body">
        <div class="checkpoint__title">Paper screening against a points-based academic record</div>
        <p>Universities score applications on a points system covering your degrees, publications, teaching experience, and research contributions before shortlisting anyone for the next stage.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="checkpoint">
      <div class="checkpoint__num">02</div>
      <div class="checkpoint__body">
        <div class="checkpoint__title">Shortlisting for interview</div>
        <p>A department or university selection committee narrows the screened pool further, often favouring candidates with publications, prior teaching experience, and a subject specialisation that matches an open post's specific requirement.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="checkpoint">
      <div class="checkpoint__num">03</div>
      <div class="checkpoint__body">
        <div class="checkpoint__title">Selection-committee interview</div>
        <p>A panel, often including external subject experts, questions you on your research, teaching approach, and subject depth — this is where interview calm and clear explanation matter as much as the dissertation itself.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="checkpoint">
      <div class="checkpoint__num">04</div>
      <div class="checkpoint__body">
        <div class="checkpoint__title">Demonstration lecture, increasingly required</div>
        <p>Many universities now ask shortlisted candidates to teach a sample class in front of the department before an offer is confirmed — a stage that tests classroom communication, not just research depth.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Even after all four stages, a confirmed offer depends on an actual sanctioned vacancy existing in
    your specific sub-field, at a university you can realistically relocate to. A brilliant record in
    a niche you specialised in can sit unmatched to any open post for a long stretch, simply because
    of subject-fit and geography.
  </p>

  <h2 id="multi-stage">The competition that restarts after your viva</h2>

  <p>
    Two tracks on this list share a pattern the "just clear NET and finish the PhD" framing hides
    completely: clearing the well-known filter does not end the competition. It restarts it, in a
    different form, right when you expect to finally be done.
  </p>

  <div class="stage-grid">
    {stageComparison.map((item) => (
      <div class="stage-card">
        <div class="stage-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="stage-card__block">
