        </div>
        <div class="stage-card__block">
          <div class="stage-card__block-title">{item.stage2Title}</div>
          <p>{item.stage2}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    This is a genuinely different caution from ordinary "hiring is selective," which shows up in
    think-tank and consulting recruitment too. There, one strong published portfolio can open the
    door, and your growth from there depends mostly on the work you keep producing. In academia and
    civil services, a second, separate filter — a scarce permanent-post market on one side, an age
    ceiling and a three-stage exam on the other — sits between "I finished the hard part" and "I
    actually reached the role and pay I pictured."
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">What AI is doing to political science research</h2>

  <p>
    Every track on this list runs on reading, writing, and analysis — exactly the layer AI tools are
    changing fastest. The honest picture has a real risk side and a real opportunity side, and for a
    PhD holder specifically, they are not the same task.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="comparison-col comparison-col--risk">
      <div class="comparison-col__label">Where AI is already automating the work</div>
      <ul>
        <li>First-pass literature reviews and summarising long documents, judgments, or committee reports are already shifting into AI-assisted workflows at many think tanks and research teams.</li>
        <li>Routine data cleaning and basic descriptive analysis of survey or voting datasets is now faster with AI tools than doing it by hand, which reduces demand for pure data-entry-adjacent research support roles.</li>
        <li>Generic explainer writing on well-covered topics is easier to automate than original fieldwork or a genuinely new argument, so purely summarising commentary carries more exposure than original analysis.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="comparison-col comparison-col--grow">
      <div class="comparison-col__label">Where AI is genuinely creating leverage</div>
      <ul>
        <li>AI-assisted literature review: feeding a large body of prior research into an AI tool for a fast first-pass synthesis, then spending your real time on the original contribution and judgment call that only your specialisation can make.</li>
        <li>Faster brief and manuscript drafting: turning field notes, interview transcripts, or a dissertation chapter into a client-ready policy brief or a public explainer far faster than before, provided you personally verify every claim before it goes out under your name.</li>
        <li>A named specialist with real depth can now run a serious research newsletter or explainer platform with AI-assisted editing and research support that used to need a small editorial team — a direct multiplier on Track 1's ownership advantage.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>Right now:</strong> use AI tools to speed up literature synthesis and first drafts, but
    verify every citation, statistic, and quoted source manually before it appears in a published
    brief, article, or academic submission — a factual error under your name is a credibility problem
    a PhD is specifically supposed to prevent.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>As adoption matures:</strong> the differentiated value shifts further toward original
    fieldwork, primary data you collect yourself, and client-specific judgment that a general-purpose
    AI tool cannot originate. That is a strong argument for keeping one active research or data
    project running at all times, rather than treating the dissertation as a finished, closed
    chapter.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">How to choose your track: The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</h2>

  <p>
    Before you commit further years — an ad-hoc teaching contract, a UPSC attempt, an unpaid
    international fellowship, or a leap into consulting — run the track you are leaning toward through
    <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>. It will not hand you a single correct answer. It will
    surface the honest trade-offs a rank list, a placement office, or a family opinion never will.
  </p>

