  <h2 id="skills">Skills that matter after a PhD</h2>

  <p>
    A PhD gives you a real starting depth — original research, sustained argument, and subject
    command. What turns that depth into a genuine high-income skill portfolio, and eventually earlier
    financial freedom, is a deliberate stack built on top of the dissertation, not the degree title
    alone.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Translation writing</strong> — turning a dissertation chapter or a journal argument into a policy brief, an op-ed, or a client memo that a non-specialist can actually use. This is the single biggest gap between a PhD holder who gets hired quickly and one who does not.</li>
    <li><strong>Data literacy</strong> — comfort with survey data, government datasets, and basic quantitative methods, even for a primarily qualitative researcher. This is the multiplier skill that separates a median policy hire from a strong one, and it is exactly what AI-assisted research now rewards.</li>
    <li><strong>Public speaking and media handling</strong> — explaining your specialisation clearly on a panel, in an interview, or in a classroom. This is the proof format think tanks, media, and hiring committees all test in some form.</li>
    <li><strong>Negotiation and stakeholder management</strong> — the human layer behind consulting, international-body work, and civil services that AI does not touch. Client and institutional trust stay entirely human.</li>
    <li><strong>A working foreign language relevant to your specialisation</strong> — a genuine differentiator for international-body and multilateral-organisation roles specifically, less critical for the domestic tracks.</li>
    <li><strong>Practical AI fluency</strong> — using AI tools to speed up literature review and drafting while personally verifying every output, per the AI section above.</li>
  </ul>

